Figure 3 illustrates that Nano-11, when co-injected with antigen, induces a transient local inflammatory response at the injection site, characterized by the recruitment of various immune cells. Part (a) shows that the numbers of Mac-2+ macrophages, antigen-presenting cells, and Ly6C+ monocytes increase and peak around day 2 post-injection, before gradually declining by day 21, while neutrophils show an early, rapid peak that quickly resolves. Part (b) provides confocal images demonstrating that these recruited cells, including MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ cells, actively phagocytose Nano-11, indicating its efficient uptake by key antigen-presenting cells at the site of injection.